p.src:
The Christoffel symbols contain one derivative of the metric: $\Gamma^\sigma_{\tau\nu}
 =
 \frac12 g^{\sigma\delta}
 \left(
 \partial_\tau g_{\delta\nu}
 +\partial_\nu g_{\delta\tau}
 -\partial_\delta g_{\tau\nu}
 \right).$ They are not tensors, so they cannot by themselves provide the left-hand side. The Riemann curvature tensor is a tensor: $R^\rho{}_{\sigma\mu\nu}
 =
 \partial_\mu\Gamma^\rho_{\nu\sigma}
 -\partial_\nu\Gamma^\rho_{\mu\sigma}
 +\Gamma^\rho_{\mu\lambda}\Gamma^\lambda_{\nu\sigma}
 -\Gamma^\rho_{\nu\lambda}\Gamma^\lambda_{\mu\sigma}.$ Its first two terms contain second derivatives of the metric; its last two contain products of first derivatives. This is exactly the differential order suggested by the Newtonian equation.

